1
00:00:01,199 --> 00:00:05,418
UltraQuant: four-bit KV caching for context-heavy agents.

2
00:00:05,468 --> 00:00:12,947
This is a short tour of the idea, and of one fused decode kernel that makes speculative decoding cheap on a four-bit cache.

3
00:00:14,697 --> 00:00:23,451
When a language model generates text, each new token attends to every earlier token, so the GPU must read the whole key-value cache from memory.

4
00:00:23,501 --> 00:00:30,010
That is almost no arithmetic per byte: about one floating point operation for every byte read in half precision.

5
00:00:30,060 --> 00:00:39,485
To keep its matrix units busy, an MI300X needs roughly one hundred sixty operations per byte, and a B300 about two hundred seventy.

6
00:00:39,535 --> 00:00:42,202
These ratios come from our measured peaks.

7
00:00:42,252 --> 00:00:47,745
So decode waits on memory, and for long-context agents the KV cache is the bottleneck.

8
00:00:47,795 --> 00:00:50,563
Fewer bytes means faster tokens.

9
00:00:52,313 --> 00:01:00,572
UltraQuant stores each value as a four-bit float, E2M1: one sign bit, two exponent bits, one mantissa bit.

10
00:01:00,622 --> 00:01:06,363
Every group of thirty-two values shares one eight-bit power-of-two scale, called UE8M0.

11
00:01:06,413 --> 00:01:14,807
For a head dimension of one hundred twenty-eight, that is sixty-four bytes of values plus four bytes of scales: sixty-eight bytes per head and token.

12
00:01:14,857 --> 00:01:20,285
Half precision needs two hundred fifty-six, and FP8 needs one hundred twenty-eight.

13
00:01:20,335 --> 00:01:24,284
That is about three point eight times smaller than half precision.

14
00:01:26,034 --> 00:01:31,921
Speculative decoding and multi-token prediction verify K candidate tokens in a single step.

15
00:01:31,971 --> 00:01:38,177
A straightforward kernel treats them as K independent queries, and each one re-reads every page of the cache.

16
00:01:38,227 --> 00:01:43,086
At K equals eight, that is eight times the memory traffic for the very same data.

17
00:01:43,136 --> 00:01:45,615
Our fused kernel reads each page once.

18
00:01:45,665 --> 00:01:53,303
The query rows for all K tokens are stacked along the M dimension of the matrix multiply, so one loaded tile serves every token.

19
00:01:53,353 --> 00:01:57,876
Same pages, same four-bit dequantisation, one pass.

20
00:01:59,626 --> 00:02:06,934
Here are measurements at batch eight, thirty-two thousand tokens of context, sixty-four query heads and eight KV heads.

21
00:02:06,984 --> 00:02:18,691
On an MI300X, at K equals eight, the fused kernel takes 0.54 milliseconds, versus 2.22 for independent queries: 4.1 times faster.

22
00:02:18,741 --> 00:02:28,394
On a B300, after tuning, K equals eight takes 0.42 milliseconds, versus 2.85: 6.8 times faster.

23
00:02:28,444 --> 00:02:32,210
At K equals one there is nothing to share, so the two match.

24
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:35,579
The gain grows with K, because page reads are shared.

25
00:02:35,629 --> 00:02:43,202
Tuning mattered: bit-trick dequantisation, a tuned warp count and pipeline depth, and sixteen-way KV splits.

26
00:02:44,952 --> 00:02:46,926
What does this mean in practice?

27
00:02:46,976 --> 00:02:54,669
A long-context agent keeps more history in the same memory, and speculative verification stops multiplying the cost of reading it.

28
00:02:54,719 --> 00:02:56,558
Now the honest caveats.

29
00:02:56,608 --> 00:03:09,871
On MI300X, which is gfx942, FP4 is dequantised in software; the paper's native scaled matrix instructions live on CDNA4, gfx950.

30
00:03:09,921 --> 00:03:21,618
Our kernels reach about half a terabyte to one terabyte per second of FP4 bytes, well below the HBM peaks of three point nine and six point four terabytes per second, so there is headroom.

31
00:03:21,668 --> 00:03:25,109
Outputs match the baseline to about one in ten thousand.

32
00:03:25,159 --> 00:03:32,872
The paper is arXiv 2606.20474, and the code and results are in the repository.
